2025-11-29 13:18:12,646 - INFO - [LLM] Final Summary: Here’s what that message indicates and how to proceed.

What actions were performed
- An autonomous agent was running a loop of “think \u2192 call a tool \u2192 observe result \u2192 decide next step.”
- It repeated that cycle until it reached the configured ceiling for how many cycles it is allowed to run.
- Because it hit that ceiling before producing a final, successful outcome, the framework halted the run and emitted “Agent stopped due to max iterations.”

Which tools were used
- The message doesn’t list the specific tools the agent invoked, and I don’t have the run trace here. In an MCP-based setup, tools are whatever capabilities you exposed (for example: filesystem read/write, HTTP requests, Git operations, shell commands, database queries, cloud APIs, etc.).
- To know exactly which tools were used and in what order, check the run logs or tool traces in your environment (e.g., the agent’s step history, debug logs, or the MCP server logs). Most frameworks record each tool call with its name, inputs, and outputs.

What the results mean
- “Stopped due to max iterations” is a safety cut-off, not necessarily a tool failure. It means the agent did not reach a “done” state within the allowed number of steps.
- The agent may have partial work completed (e.g., intermediate files created, partial responses, or fetched data), but it did not finalize or return a success result before hitting the limit.

Why any errors occurred
Common causes include:
- Ambiguous or missing success criteria: The agent couldn’t tell when the task was complete, so it kept looping.
- Blocked progress: A tool call failed repeatedly (permissions, credentials, rate limits, network errors) and the agent kept retrying or pivoting without resolving the root issue.
- Too tight limits: The task legitimately needs more steps than the current iteration cap.
- Missing inputs or context: The agent lacked a key parameter, file, or access token and never obtained it.
- Circular strategy: The agent oscillated between approaches (e.g., search \u2192 parse \u2192 search again) without converging.

What you should do next
Pick the items that best fit your setup:

- Review the run trace:
  - Inspect the step-by-step log to see the last few actions, tool names, inputs, outputs, and any error messages.
  - Identify the point where progress stalled or repeated.

- Tighten the task definition:
  - Provide explicit success criteria (e.g., “Produce X file at path Y containing Z; stop once it exists and passes validation A.”).
  - Supply missing inputs (credentials, API keys, filenames, endpoints, schemas).

- Resolve tool issues:
  - If you see permission or auth errors, fix credentials/permissions and rerun.
  - If you see rate limits/timeouts, add backoff, caching, or widen timeouts.

- Adjust agent limits and settings:
  - Increase the iteration/step limit (e.g., max_iterations, max_steps, or similar parameter in your framework).
  - Raise token or time budgets if the agent was truncated mid-reasoning.
  - Add a hard stop condition so the agent knows when to finish (e.g., a validation check or “return final answer now” instruction after a milestone).

- Reduce complexity:
  - Break the task into smaller sub-tasks the agent can complete in fewer steps.
  - Provide a short, suggested plan so the agent doesn’t waste steps exploring.

- Rerun with better observability:
  - Enable debug logging for tool calls.
  - Capture artifacts (files produced, API responses) so you can confirm intermediate success.

If you can share the last 10–20 steps of the log (tool names, inputs, outputs, and any errors), I can pinpoint exactly where it stalled and give targeted fixes.
